    month: 'short',
    day: 'numeric'
  })
</script>

# Cookies

<p class="text-muted-foreground text-sm">{formattedDate}</p>

There was supposed to be an article here, but everything has [already been said](https://www-test.ics.uci.edu/~fielding/pubs/dissertation/evaluation.htm#sec_6_3_4_2).

Cookies are evil. They serve no purpose other than violating privacy and compromising security.

<details class="group mt-12">
  <summary class="text-muted-foreground flex cursor-pointer text-sm list-none items-center gap-1 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
    Vulnerabilities introduced by cookies
    <ChevronDown class="size-4 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" />
  </summary>
  <ol class="timeline">
    <li>
      <time>1994</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Supercookies</strong>
        The original dot-counting rule let any site set a cookie for all of <code>.co.uk</code>. Patched by the Public Suffix List (2007), which is maintained by hand.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP_cookie#Supercookie">Supercookie<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://publicsuffix.org/learn/">Public Suffix List<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>1996</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Cross-site tracking</strong>
        Third-party cookies follow the user across sites. Blocked by Safari (2020), partitioned by Firefox (2022); Chrome gave up in 2024.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0105018">HTTP Cookies: Standards, Privacy, and Politics<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://webkit.org/blog/10218/full-third-party-cookie-blocking-and-more/">Safari<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://blog.mozilla.org/security/2021/02/23/total-cookie-protection/">Firefox<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://privacysandbox.google.com/blog/privacy-sandbox-update">Chrome<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2000</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Cookie theft via XSS</strong>
        Any script on the page reads <code>document.cookie</code>. Patched by <code>HttpOnly</code> (2002), opt-in.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20020124063448/http://www.cert.org/advisories/CA-2000-02.html">CERT CA-2000-02<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://owasp.org/www-community/HttpOnly">HttpOnly<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2001</time>
      <div>
        <strong>CSRF</strong>
        Any site makes the browser send an authenticated request. Patched by tokens in every framework, then <code>SameSite</code> (2016), on by default in Chromium browsers since 2020.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://seclists.org/bugtraq/2001/Jun/217">Bugtraq<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://web.dev/articles/samesite-cookies-explained">SameSite cookies explained<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2002</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Session fixation</strong>
        The attacker plants a known session ID before login. Patched in application code.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.acrossecurity.com/papers/session_fixation.pdf">Session Fixation Vulnerability in Web-based Applications<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2003</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Cross-site tracing</strong>
        The <code>TRACE</code> method echoes the cookie back to a script, bypassing <code>HttpOnly</code>. Browsers banned <code>TRACE</code>.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Cross_Site_Tracing">OWASP<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2006</time>
      <div>
        <strong>XSSI and JSONP leaks</strong>
        A <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag pointing to another origin carries cookies and leaks the authenticated response. Patched by unparseable prefixes, then CORB (2018).
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://blog.jeremiahgrossman.com/2006/01/advanced-web-attack-techniques-using.html">Advanced Web Attack Techniques using GMail<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.chromium.org/Home/chromium-security/corb-for-developers/">CORB<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2007</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Cross-site leaks</strong>
        Timing, errors, and frame counts of authenticated cross-site responses reveal the user's state. Mitigated by Fetch Metadata (2019), CORP, and COOP (2020), all opt-in.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://crypto.stanford.edu/~dabo/abstracts/webtiming.html">Exposing Private Information by Timing Web Applications<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://xsleaks.dev/">XS-Leaks Wiki<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-fetch-metadata/">Fetch Metadata<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2008</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Clickjacking</strong>
        A framed page is still authenticated, so a disguised click acts as the user. Patched by <code>X-Frame-Options</code> (2009), opt-in.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clickjacking">Wikipedia<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/archive/blogs/ie/ie8-security-part-vii-clickjacking-defenses">IE8 Security: ClickJacking Defenses<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2008</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Login CSRF</strong>
        The attacker logs the victim into the attacker's account. Patched in application code.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://seclab.stanford.edu/websec/csrf/">Robust Defenses for Cross-Site Request Forgery<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2010</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Client-side trust</strong>
        State kept in a cookie is attacker-controlled input: ASP.NET's encrypted cookies fell to a padding oracle, unsigned and serialized ones are simply edited. Patched in application code.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security-updates/securitybulletins/2010/ms10-070">MS10-070<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padding_oracle_attack">Padding oracle attack<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2010</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Sidejacking</strong>
        Firesheep hijacks sessions on open Wi-Fi with one click. <code>Secure</code> had existed since 1994, unused. Patched by HSTS (2012), opt-in.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firesheep">Firesheep<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6797">RFC 6797<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2011–2014</time>
      <div>
        <strong>BEAST, CRIME, BREACH, POODLE</strong>
        The same secret is resent with every request, so it can be guessed byte by byte. Patched by removing TLS compression and SSL 3.0; BREACH is only mitigated.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transport_Layer_Security#BEAST_attack">BEAST<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CRIME">CRIME<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BREACH">BREACH<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/POODLE">POODLE<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2013</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Cookie tossing</strong>
        A subdomain or a plain HTTP response overwrites the cookies of an HTTPS site. Patched by the <code>__Host-</code> prefix (2016), opt-in by cookie name.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://github.blog/engineering/yummy-cookies-across-domains/">Yummy cookies across domains<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.usenix.org/conference/usenixsecurity15/technical-sessions/presentation/zheng">Cookies Lack Integrity<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2013</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Cross-site WebSocket hijacking</strong>
        The handshake carries cookies and is exempt from the same-origin policy. Patched in application code.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://christian-schneider.net/CrossSiteWebSocketHijacking.html">Cross-Site WebSocket Hijacking<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2014</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Cookie bomb</strong>
        Oversized cookies make the server reject every request. Not patched.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://homakov.blogspot.com/2014/01/cookie-bomb-or-lets-break-internet.html">Cookie Bomb or let's break the Internet<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2016</time>
      <div>
        <strong>CORS with credentials</strong>
        A server that reflects <code>Origin</code> and allows credentials lets any site read authenticated responses. Patched in application code.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://portswigger.net/research/exploiting-cors-misconfigurations-for-bitcoins-and-bounties">Exploiting CORS misconfigurations for Bitcoins and bounties<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>2023</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Prefix bypass</strong>
        Browsers and servers parse the <code>Cookie</code> header differently, so a tossed cookie passes for a <code>__Host-</code> one. Patched per framework.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.usenix.org/conference/usenixsecurity23/presentation/squarcina">Cookie Crumbles<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <time>now</time>
      <div>
        <strong>Session replay</strong>
        A cookie is a bearer token: malware copies it and skips the second factor. Device Bound Session Credentials shipped in Chrome for Windows in 2026.
        <ul>
        <li><a href="https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1539/">MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1539<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        <li><a href="https://blog.google/security/protecting-cookies-with-device-bound-session-credentials/">Device Bound Session Credentials<ExternalLink class="size-3" /></a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>
</details>

<style>
  .timeline {
    --year: 6.5rem;
    --gap: 2.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .timeline li {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: var(--year) 1fr;
    column-gap: var(--gap);
    padding-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .timeline li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    left: calc(var(--year) + var(--gap) / 2 - 1px);
    width: 2px;
    background: var(--muted);
  }

  .timeline li:first-child::before {
    top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .timeline li:last-child::before {
    bottom: auto;
    height: 0.75rem;
  }

  .timeline li::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 0.75rem;
    left: calc(var(--year) + var(--gap) / 2);
    width: 0.625rem;
    height: 0.625rem;
    border: 2px solid var(--background);
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--muted-foreground);
    box-sizing: content-box;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  }

  .timeline time {
    color: var(--muted-foreground);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.5rem;
    text-align: right;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .timeline strong {
    display: block;
  }

  .timeline ul {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0 1rem;
    margin-top: 0.25rem;
    padding-left: 0;
    list-style: none;
    color: var(--muted-foreground);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  .timeline ul a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.25rem;
  }

  .timeline ul li {
    display: block;
    padding-bottom: 0;
  }

  .timeline ul li::before,
  .timeline ul li::after {
    content: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .timeline {
      --year: 3rem;
      --gap: 1.75rem;
    }

    .timeline time {
      white-space: normal;
    }
  }
</style>
